    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.tree_decomposition - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.tree_decomposition.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.tree_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a tree decomposition and an upper bound on the treewidth.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.tree_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a tree decomposition and an upper bound on the treewidth.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.tree_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find a tree decomposition and an upper bound on the treewidth.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind a tree decomposition and an upper bound on the treewidth.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L77976-L78095){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.tree_decomposition(heuristic=\"min_fill_in\")\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Tree decomposition](../assets/graphs/tree-decomposition.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a five-node cycle A to E; the dashed accent chords B–E and C–E split it into three triangles. Right: the returned tree of bags, each bag a shaded box holding one triangle: {A,B,E}, {B,C,E}, and {C,D,E}, joined in a chain; neighboring bags share two nodes, and every cycle edge lies inside a bag. The largest bag has 3 nodes, so the card gives the width 3 − 1 = 2.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA tree decomposition arranges the nodes into overlapping groups called *bags*, connected\nas a tree, so that every edge lies inside some bag and the bags containing any given\nnode form a connected piece of the tree. Its *width* is the size of the largest bag\nminus one, and the treewidth of the graph is the smallest width possible: trees have\ntreewidth 1, cycles 2, and a complete graph on $n$ nodes $n - 1$. Many NP-hard problems\n(coloring, independent sets, probabilistic inference in graphical models) become fast\non graphs of small treewidth when solved bag by bag along the decomposition.\n\nComputing the exact treewidth is NP-hard, so this method uses a greedy elimination\nheuristic, as NetworkX does, and the width it reports is an upper bound:\n\n- `\"min_fill_in\"` (default): eliminate the node whose neighbors need the fewest extra\n  edges to become a clique. Usually tighter.\n- `\"min_degree\"`: eliminate a node of smallest degree. Faster.\n\nEdge direction, self-loops and parallel edges are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[heuristic]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;min_fill_in&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   `\"min_fill_in\"` (default) or `\"min_degree\"`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[int, Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The width, and the decomposition as a new undirected tree whose nodes are the bags.\n    Each bag is labeled with its members, such as `\"{A,B,C}\"`, and stores their node\n    IDs in the node attribute `\"bag\"`. An empty graph has width `-1` and a single empty\n    bag.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `heuristic=` is unknown.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nElimination repeatedly picks a node by the heuristic, joins its remaining neighbors\ninto a clique (the \"fill-in\" edges), records the node with its neighbors, and removes\nit; elimination stops once the remaining nodes form a clique, which becomes the first\nbag. Undoing the eliminations in reverse, each node and its recorded neighbors form a\nnew bag, attached to an existing bag that contains those neighbors. Min-degree uses a\nheap, while min-fill-in recomputes fill-in counts for every node at each step, so it\nis slower, roughly $O(V^2 \\Delta^2)$ for maximum degree $\\Delta$. Ties are broken by\nnode ID; the widths match NetworkX's.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~chordal_graph_treewidth`) \n\n:   The exact treewidth of a chordal graph.\n\n[](`~junction_tree`) \n\n:   The decomposition used for inference in graphical models.\n\n[](`~is_chordal`) \n\n:   Check whether the graph has an exact tree of cliques.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA cycle has treewidth 2, and its decomposition is a chain of bags of three nodes:\n\n::: {#ccc438a5 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nring = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"E\", \"A\")]\n)\nwidth, tree = ring.tree_decomposition()\n\nwidth, [tree.get_label_from_id(n) for n in tree.get_node_ids()]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(2, ['{B,C,E}', '{A,B,E}', '{C,D,E}'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Molecules, bag by bag\n\n`molecules()` holds the graphs of seven small molecules, one node per atom (leaving out\nthe hydrogens) and one edge per bond. A molecule without rings is a tree, of treewidth 1,\nand its rings raise the width only to 2, even in caffeine with its two fused rings. With\nbags this small, problems that are hard on most graphs can be solved quickly on molecules,\none bag at a time:\n\n::: {#28cc6f35 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nmolecules = ec.molecules()\n\npl.DataFrame(\n    {\n        \"molecule\": list(molecules),\n        \"atoms\": [g.count_nodes() for g in molecules.values()],\n        \"width\": [g.tree_decomposition()[0] for g in molecules.values()],\n    }\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (7, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>molecule</th><th>atoms</th><th>width</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;benzene&quot;</td><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;cyclohexane&quot;</td><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;ethanol&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;dimethyl_ether&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;butane&quot;</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;isobutane&quot;</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;caffeine&quot;</td><td>14</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach bag of caffeine's decomposition holds at most three atoms, and neighboring bags\nshare the atoms between them:\n\n::: {#37169b32 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nwidth, tree = molecules[\"caffeine\"].tree_decomposition()\n\ntree.plot(label_by=\"label\", seed=1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"5.6 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"5.6\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"688.8\" height=\"492.0\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"461.0\" y1=\"122.2\" x2=\"468.1\" y2=\"136.5\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"2.2800000000000002\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"461.0\" y1=\"122.2\" x2=\"468.1\" y2